  "summary": "Large, multi-story, communal residences built by Hakka people in mountainous regions of Fujian Province.",
  "description": "Circular or square earthen structures with thick walls, internal courtyards, and living quarters arranged in concentric circles. These fortified communities were designed to protect Hakka families from conflicts while maintaining communal living.",
  "culturalContext": "Fujian's Hakka tulou — UNESCO-listed rammed-earth roundhouses holding up to 800 people — are China's most communal vernacular: 3-5 story earthen rings around ancestral-hall courtyards, one gate, no exterior windows at ground level, bedrooms radiating outward with kitchens below. Built over centuries by Hakka migrants for clan defense and collective living, they astounded Cold-War satellites (mistaken for missile silos); Chengqi Lou and Tianluokeng cluster anchor Fujian's \"earthen castle\" tourism.",
